// The signature under the title block — Ben's own hand.
//
// HOW THIS IS BUILT, because it is not the obvious thing.
//
// The mark is drawn as the OUTLINE of the ink, filled — not as a stroked line.
// That is what preserves the pen: the real signature swells and thins as the
// nib changes speed and pressure (14 to 30 units wide across this mark), and a
// uniform stroke throws all of that away and reads as wire.
//
// A filled outline cannot be animated with stroke-dashoffset, though — there is
// no line to draw. So the fill is revealed through a mask containing the
// CENTRELINE of each stroke, drawn thick enough to cover the ink. Animating the
// dash on those mask strokes sweeps the reveal along the path the pen actually
// travelled, and the ink appears at its true varying width as it goes.
//
// Both halves come from the photograph Ben sent (Assets/WhatsApp Image
// 2026-07-29…): thresholded to ink, the boundary traced for the outline and the
// same ink skeletonised for the centrelines.
//
// The six reveal paths are the signature in writing order, and the engine in
// Page.astro strokes them 0.22s apart — so path order IS the order the hand
// moves. Reordering them reorders the writing.
//
// The reveal strokes are ORDINARY strokes, not the vector-effect:
// non-scaling-stroke the rest of the site's marks use, because the mask has to
// stay locked to the ink at any size. Page.astro measures the dash in whichever
// unit the stroke is actually drawn in.
//
// signature.src in src/data/site.js still switches this to an <img> for a
// flatbed scan, if one ever supersedes the photo — but a raster signature is a
// flat image and does NOT animate.
import { signature } from '../data/site.js';
import { u } from '../lib/url.js';

interface Props {
  class?: string;
}

const { class: className = '', ...rest } = Astro.props;

